**  What is Collection Framework ?
   - Collection<T> is the root interface of the Java Collections Framework.
   - It extends Iterable<T>, meaning all collections can be iterated using Iterator or forEach().
   - Implemented by: List, Set, and Queue.

   java.lang.Iterable<T>
      ├── java.util.Collection<T>  (Main Interface)
      │      ├── List<T>    (Ordered, allows duplicates)
      │      ├── Set<T>     (Unordered, unique elements)
      │      ├── Queue<T>   (FIFO, used for processing)
      └── java.util.Map<K, V>  (Key-value pairs, NOT part of Collection)


3. Key Features of Collection<T>
✅ Supports storing multiple objects dynamically.
✅ Includes basic operations like add(), remove(), contains(), size(), etc.
✅ Allows conversion to arrays using toArray().
✅ Supports iteration via Iterator and forEach().


EXAMPLE :-

Public Class collectionsInterface{

Collection<String> collectionFramework = new ArrayList<>();

CollectionFramework.add("Anand");
CollectionFramework.add("Kulkarni");

CollectionFramework.forEach(printname -> System.out.println(printname));

}





